{"title":"Adding fractions with different denominators","key_idea":"To add fractions, you must first make the bottom numbers the same, then add the top numbers.","method":["Find the least common multiple of the two denominators.","Multiply the numerator and denominator of each fraction by the number needed to reach that common denominator.","Add the numerators and keep the common denominator the same.","Simplify the answer if possible."],"worked_example":{"problem":"Add 2/3 and 1/6.","steps":["The denominators are 3 and 6; the least common multiple is 6.","Multiply 2/3 by 2/2 to get 4/6.","The second fraction 1/6 already has denominator 6.","Add the numerators: 4 + 1 = 5, so the answer is 5/6."],"answer":"5/6"},"key_terms":[{"term":"denominator","meaning":"the bottom number of a fraction"},{"term":"numerator","meaning":"the top number of a fraction"},{"term":"least common multiple","meaning":"the smallest number that both denominators divide into evenly"}],"common_mistakes":["Adding the numerators and denominators together instead of finding a common denominator first.","Forgetting to multiply both the top and bottom of the fraction by the same number."],"remember":"Always make the bottoms the same before you add the tops.","quick_check":[{"q":"Add 1/2 and 1/4.","a":"3/4"},{"q":"Add 2/5 and 1/10.","a":"1/2"}],"tell_your_child":"When the bottom numbers are different, you cannot add the fractions right away. First, rewrite both fractions so their bottoms match, then add them together."}
